    "answer": {
      "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{1}{4y(y^{2}-1)}$ for $x>0$. Apply the chain rule twice, then use $y^{2}-1=\\sqrt{x}$.",
      "worked": "\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(1+\\sqrt{x})^{1/2}\\\\y'&=\\frac12(1+\\sqrt{x})^{-1/2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{x}}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{1}{4y(y^{2}-1)},\\end{align*}$$\n\n since $y=\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{x}}$ and $y^{2}-1=\\sqrt{x}$, for $x>0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
